PASS level_intents: all 120 levels have a specific intent
PASS no_filler_levels: no trivial filler levels after the tutorial
FAIL level_design: level-design review 7.4/10 (bar 8), 0 level(s) below 6/10: A 120-level set that is strong on teaching structure. Each city adds one rule, then a boss, then a mastery send-off. Most boards have a real "aha", often a counter-intuitive "what NOT to build" insight. The set has four main weaknesses. (1) Many levels repeat one template: the obvious line is too long, so split it, with the carriage, hill, snow or wave only changing the numbers. (2) Several boards are trivial or contradict their own intent. (3) The late-game difficulty is mostly tuned to the targets, but several bosses and the finale sit near the floor (L120 casual 39%, L102 47%, L113 47%). The L120 casual rate is also a lone cliff against skilled 77%. (4) Pacing has too many 4-5 level stretches of the same lesson before a new mechanic. Fairness is good, since most levels have a stated trap and trap rate 0. The ones whose purpose conflicts with their board are listed below. Curve: The ramp is clean and well-paced early. L1-3 teach lines, transfers and short lines at 87-97% casual. L4-12 add construction sites and combine them, with a breather at L7 and a boss at L11. Cities then each introduce one mechanic alone: tunnels (13), carriages (25), hills (37), ferries (49), snow (61), markets/interchange (73), loops (85), waves (97), heritage (109). Each city follows the same rhythm: teach, practise, twist, combine, boss, breather, mastery. That predictability is a strength for a calm game, but it makes the second half feel formulaic. The "split the long line, put the busy pair on one line, keep a track" insight recurs in nearly every city. The new mechanics (hills, snow, waves) mostly just reduce seat counts. Difficulty targets: casual win rates fall steadily to 39-60% in the last third, which is on target. The skilled rates are fine except for a few bosses and the finale, which sit at 77-86%, and L62 (73% casual / 77% skilled) looks like a mismatch. Breathers are sometimes not easier than their neighbours: L31 and L19 are fine, but L67 and L91 rely on drawing puzzles. Stretches of 90%+ casual are limited to L3, L13, L19, L43, L91 and L97, so the easy-run limit is respected. Recommendation: cut or merge about 8 filler levels, add a distinct new texture (a time-based or multi-glyph twist) in cities 7-10, and soften L120, the last city's bosses and the late-game casual spikes (L102, L113).
REDESIGN THESE LEVELS (keep the ones not listed):
L8 (6/10): The aha 'skip B' relies on a station that sends nobody. Same trick as L32 and L76. The 'late stations at both ends' idea overlaps L4 and L6. → Make B carry some demand so skipping it costs something, or replace this level with a late station that tempts a loop.
L40 (6/10): It stacks three rules and the aha has two answers (the flat lane or the climb), so it doesn't commit to one insight. → Remove the flat lane option or the third line.
L59 (6/10): Casual 58%. The aha has too many moving parts (tunnel, boat, carriage, card, late station) and the card is not decisive. → Remove the weekly card.
L62 (6/10): Casual 73% / skilled 77% is an unfair-feeling gap, and the aha is hidden. The Frostholm reflex is a trap only a skilled player sees. → Add the forecast hint and soften the snow weeks.
L71 (6/10): Casual 61% / skilled 83%. The aha duplicates L35's stadium split and the prior city's 'both lines on' answer scrapes a win, so it is not decisive. → Make the both-lines answer fail.
L74 (6/10): A repeat of L61 / L25 with market spikes. → Add a second spike in a different week.
L83 (6/10): Casual 63% / skilled 85%. All choices win, so the card choice is only a star. The 3rd star feels like a guess. → Make one card fail.
L88 (6/10): Casual 65% / skilled 79% is a poor pairing. A messy 3-rule combination, and the trap is hard to see. → Remove the hill and keep loop plus late station.
L91 (6/10): A breather with 8 tracks and 2 lines on a T. The 'loops allowed' mechanic is not used. → Add a ring option.
L102 (6/10): Casual 47% / skilled 83% is harsh for a mid-city boss and the key-shaped board is a copy of L89 / L90. → Raise the goal margin.
L107 (6/10): Casual 52% / skilled 79%. Stacks wave, snow, loop, carriage and quiet stops, and the trap fails in week 3. → Remove one rule.
L113 (6/10): Casual 47% for a tunnel+loop combine is too hard, and the heritage side is a minor add-on. → Raise the goal margin.
L119 (6/10): Casual 57%. The aha has three outs and a trap, hard to read. → Remove one of the outs.
L120 (6/10): Casual 39% / skilled 77%. Far below the other levels, so it is a cliff. The tower swap and the card are all from earlier levels. → Raise the casual win rate to ~50-55% by loosening the goal (389 of 442 is 88%) to about 80%, or remove the calm card.
L6 (7/10): Casual 81% is the lowest of the early levels, and the 8 first moves make it noisy. The snake trap is similar to L5, L11 and L12. → Remove the late station G so it tests only split plus shared stop. Keep G for L11.
L7 (7/10): A pure drawing puzzle. The dead-end rule is not explained by earlier levels, and the 3rd star is trivial for skilled players. → Make the 3rd star 'use one line' with a visible end-cap hint on F.
L10 (7/10): The 'split where the riders are' idea is a close sibling of L5 and L9. 2 tracks to spare for the 3rd star is a harsh ask on top. → Drop the spare-track star to 1 spare, or swap in a different 3rd star.
L11 (7/10): Boss at 76% casual feels easy for a boss. It is mostly a repeat of L6's snake trap, and the card safety net removes the pressure. → Add a second late station or drop the +1 line safety net so the end-in-G decision is not forgiven.
L14 (7/10): The trap is simply 'cross at the middle'. Close to L13 in structure. → Give the middle pier a larger demand so the straight crossing looks tempting for a reason.
L16 (7/10): It mixes three rules. It also tells the player the trap in the text, because the demand cards show F is the one that matters, and it must be explained. → Keep it as the first card level in the tunnel city but remove one of the three rules.
L18 (7/10): The boss is a repeat of L16 and L4 (save the scarce thing for a late station). The 6-edge layout is cluttered. → Add a second late station so the choice is not binary.
L19 (7/10): A breather with an optional tunnel is fine. 93% casual is OK. → None.
L21 (7/10): Aha and trap are very literal: 'spend both'. The 7-hop trap sounds contrived. → Make the land bridge tempting by giving it a cheap track cost.
L23 (7/10): Casual 66% is steep for a level 23 and the 'both tunnels, one line' answer is not hinted. → Raise the goal margin a bit, or show the festival's forecast earlier.
L25 (7/10): A trivial board. The only decision is the carriage, so 'First Carriage' on this layout is thin. → Add a second pair of lines so the carriage is a choice.

level-design score 7.4/10 — A 120-level set that is strong on teaching structure. Each city adds one rule, then a boss, then a mastery send-off. Most boards have a real "aha", often a counter-intuitive "what NOT to build" insight. The set has four main weaknesses. (1) Many levels repeat one template: the obvious line is too long, so split it, with the carriage, hill, snow or wave only changing the numbers. (2) Several boards are trivial or contradict their own intent. (3) The late-game difficulty is mostly tuned to the targets, but several bosses and the finale sit near the floor (L120 casual 39%, L102 47%, L113 47%). The L120 casual rate is also a lone cliff against skilled 77%. (4) Pacing has too many 4-5 level stretches of the same lesson before a new mechanic. Fairness is good, since most levels have a stated trap and trap rate 0. The ones whose purpose conflicts with their board are listed below.
curve: The ramp is clean and well-paced early. L1-3 teach lines, transfers and short lines at 87-97% casual. L4-12 add construction sites and combine them, with a breather at L7 and a boss at L11. Cities then each introduce one mechanic alone: tunnels (13), carriages (25), hills (37), ferries (49), snow (61), markets/interchange (73), loops (85), waves (97), heritage (109). Each city follows the same rhythm: teach, practise, twist, combine, boss, breather, mastery. That predictability is a strength for a calm game, but it makes the second half feel formulaic. The "split the long line, put the busy pair on one line, keep a track" insight recurs in nearly every city. The new mechanics (hills, snow, waves) mostly just reduce seat counts. Difficulty targets: casual win rates fall steadily to 39-60% in the last third, which is on target. The skilled rates are fine except for a few bosses and the finale, which sit at 77-86%, and L62 (73% casual / 77% skilled) looks like a mismatch. Breathers are sometimes not easier than their neighbours: L31 and L19 are fine, but L67 and L91 rely on drawing puzzles. Stretches of 90%+ casual are limited to L3, L13, L19, L43, L91 and L97, so the easy-run limit is respected. Recommendation: cut or merge about 8 filler levels, add a distinct new texture (a time-based or multi-glyph twist) in cities 7-10, and soften L120, the last city's bosses and the late-game casual spikes (L102, L113).
